# Development Flow Skill (GitHub Flow)

Follow this end-to-end workflow for every code or documentation change.

## 1. Branch From `main`

Never commit or push directly to `main`.

1. Verify your current branch and working tree:
   ```bash
   git status
   git branch --show-current
   ```
2. Sync `main` and cut a new short-lived branch:
   ```bash
   git checkout main
   git pull --ff-only origin main
   git checkout -b <type>/<short-kebab-description>
   ```
   Recommended branch prefixes: `feat/`, `fix/`, `refactor/`, `docs/`, `test/`, `chore/`.

## 2. Implement and Document

1. Keep edits scoped to the task.
2. Follow `.agents/rules/docstring.md` for docstrings and comments.
3. Add or update tests covering the new behavior and edge cases.
4. Log terminal verification runs into `.log/` per `.agents/rules/terminal-logging.md`.

## 3. Run Pre-Commit Checks

Before committing or pushing, ensure hooks are installed and all checks pass:

```bash
pre-commit install
pre-commit run --all-files
```

If the project uses `uv`, run `uv run pre-commit install` and `uv run pre-commit run --all-files`.
Fix any formatting, linting, type-checking, or test failures. Never bypass hooks with `--no-verify`.

## 4. Commit and Push

1. Commit staged changes using Conventional Commits (see `.agents/skills/commit-and-pr/SKILL.md`).
2. Rebase onto the latest `origin/main` if needed to keep history clean.
3. Push your branch to the remote:
   ```bash
   git push -u origin "$(git branch --show-current)"
   ```

## 5. Open a Pull Request Into `main`

1. Open a Pull Request from your branch targeting **`main`**.
2. Populate the PR description using `.github/pull_request_template.md` (see `.agents/skills/commit-and-pr/SKILL.md`).
3. Confirm automated CI checks pass and address review feedback before merging.
